ll stack trace and running the 500 handler. Now `recovery.go:71` treats it the same way gin already treats a broken pipe:\n- It logs one line (the error and the request) with no stack trace.\n- It records the error on the context and aborts.\n- It doesn't call the 500 handler or a custom recovery handler, and keeps any status already set.\n\nI also added `TestPanicWithAbortHandler` in `recovery_test.go`. It checks that the status the handler set stays in place, the error message is logged, and no \"panic recovered\" stack dump appears.\n\n**One design choice:** recovery handles this panic instead of passing it on. Passing it on would let Go's server abort the connection, which is what `ErrAbortHandler` is meant to do, but it would skip gin's other middleware. Say if you'd prefer that.\n\nThe changes are on a new branch, `fix/recover-abort-handler`. To commit, set your git name and email and run:\n```\ngit commit -am \"fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover\"\n```","ttft_ms":3220,"type":"result","duration_ms":36906,"uuid":"bb14ddf6-7457-462e-b64f-f63926ed58e2","ttft_stream_ms":2697,"time_to_request_ms":150,"first_content_frame_ms":2698,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
